From 2a4d8d3a9501bf3b3fe3b78d7c6bad38bfc76064 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Penny <Pennyroyal@agentmail.to>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 19:25:15 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] perf(moe): fold the row expansion into the A0 fused
 routing prologue (donor G2-2)

Direct port of the published donor patch's input-preparation half, kept at the
text level wherever the separation from A3 does not require a change:

  aiueo52/flash-next-rtxpro6000 @ e0fa9fa9fc3ccd710a8fb3d2639b85ce62188c23
  patches/flashinfer/04-g2-2-fold-expand-rows.patch

(wheel-side flashinfer/data/csrc/... paths map 1:1 to this repo's csrc/...
paths).  expandInputRowsKernel's NVFP4 branch -- the row duplication/permute
plus the re-quantisation -- now runs inside the routing prologue launch: every
block re-runs the cheap deterministic rank, block 0 publishes the routing maps
and expert offsets, and every block stages the rows it owns out of block-local
copies of the inverse row and expert maps, so the row phase needs neither a
global round trip nor findTotalEltsLessThanTarget's binary search per row.  The
donor's register-staged row loads (G2_EXPAND_MAX_CHUNKS), the quantise loop,
the K-dim SF padding loop and the permuted-router-scale write are its, line
for line.  The standalone expandInputRowsKernelLauncher is skipped only when
the fused launch actually took the offer.

This is the item John chose to split out: the rejected stride fusion is NOT
restored.  computeStridesTmaWarpSpecialized stays a separate launch, in its
usual place behind setupTmaWarpSpecializedInputs, in the same order relative to
the prologue and GEMM1 as on the base, and the fused kernel is given no
layout_info, problem shape or weight argument at all.  The donor's row branch
consumes only the routing offsets, the inverse maps and the activation/scaling
inputs, so nothing here depends on the descriptors; no concrete dependency
forced a different design.

Port deviations (all wiring, none algorithmic):

* The donor folded the rows into its A3 kernel and switched it on with a
  FUSE_EXPAND template flag; with A3 out of the picture that kernel and the
  stride_blocks/row_blocks split do not exist, so this adds its own
  fusedBuildExpertMapsSortFirstTokenAndExpandKernel and drops the flag and the
  if-constexpr around it.  The row loop iterates blockIdx.x / gridDim.x
  directly instead of row_slot / row_blocks, and the grid is exactly
  num_tokens * experts_per_token (one block per possible permuted row) rather
  than stride_blocks + row_blocks.  The launcher is templated on
  <T, InputAct, EXPERTS_PER_TOKEN> only; WeightType/OutputType/ScaleBiasType
  were there for the descriptor branch.
* FusedPrologueRequest (moe_kernels.h) carries the donor's expand_* fields and
  the routing pointers, but no layout_info/gemm/weight/bias/quant_params.  The
  A0 kernel itself is untouched, so the accepted prologue stays bit-for-bit
  whatever the fold decides.
* The type test is std::is_same_v<T, Fp4Type>, not the donor's
  __nv_fp4_e2m1: this repo's fp4_compat.h makes Fp4Type cutlass::float_e2m1_t
  in the Hopper-CUTLASS-FP4 build, and the standalone kernel keys off Fp4Type
  too, so raw FP16/BF16 -> NVFP4 keeps the fold on whichever alias is in play.
* cudaTriggerProgrammaticLaunchCompletion sits right after the routing stores
  are issued (where the A0 kernel puts it) rather than at the end of the donor
  body: the next launch is now the independent stride setup, which reads only
  expert_first_token_offset, so releasing it early lets it overlap the rows.
  Its own cudaGridDependencySynchronize still waits for this grid, so the
  permuted rows are visible to GEMM1 before it reads them.
* Added the two row-alignment asserts the standalone launcher's host checks
  cover when it does run, and kept the donor's host gate verbatim:
  bf16/fp16 -> NVFP4 only, non-null input/output/SF pointers, hidden_size
  divisible by CVT_ELTS_PER_THREAD * 32, chunk count <= G2_EXPAND_MAX_CHUNKS,
  positive num_tokens/top-k, num_experts_per_node + 1 < 1024, num_tokens <= 32,
  top-k in {1,2,4,6,8,10}, and no FLASHINFER_USE_NVFP4_4OVER6 /
  DISABLE_FP4_QUANT_FAST_MATH (the kernel fixes those tags to keep one
  instantiation).  The caller only offers the fold where the standalone
  launcher's other arguments are inactive: use_fp4, no AWQ / W4AFP8 /
  wfp4afp8 / MXFP8 act scaling / LoRA, raw (unquantised) input, FC1 weight
  block scales and fc1_fp4_act_scale_ present.  FLASHINFER_MOE_FUSED_PROLOGUE=0
  turns the fold off too, and everything else -- including TP2/EP and
  concurrent use, which see the same expert masking and the same buffers as A0
  -- keeps the accepted routing plus the standalone expansion.  Internal
  environment switch only; no new launch flag, configurator control or doc.

Tests: new GPU check tests/moe/test_cutlass_fused_moe_g22_fold_expand.py
reuses the accepted A0/G2-1 NVFP4 harness (same 512-expert / top-10 shapes,
same subprocess-per-arm pattern, same torch reference) and pins the launched
device ops instead of trusting the flag: the FLASHINFER_MOE_FOLD_EXPAND=1 arm
must show fusedBuildExpertMapsSortFirstTokenAndExpandKernel with neither
expandInputRowsKernel nor the A0 kernel nor the 3-kernel prologue, while the
=0 arm and the >32-token shape must show the A0 prologue plus the standalone
expansion.  Both arms must also show computeStridesTmaWarpSpecializedKernel --
the separate stride launch is the point of this split, and the A3-era fused
name may never appear -- so a build that quietly skipped the stride setup, or
that folded nothing, fails rather than passing vacuously.  Per case: the two
arms must agree bit for bit, each arm must be stable across two calls into one
reused output buffer, and both must match the torch NVFP4 reference.
test_cutlass_fused_moe_a0_prologue.py's assert_path now counts the folded
kernel as the fused prologue (its kill switch is unchanged), since the fold is
on by default; the A0 kernel name is not a substring of the folded name, so
both are matched explicitly.

Review 1 follow-up (one test gap, no runtime change): the G2-2 cases went
through the G2-1 _inputs/_reference helpers, which are fixed at HIDDEN=256, so
every arm staged a single 8-element chunk per row -- trips = hidden /
(8 * 32) = 1 -- while the Flash-Next width is 2560 (ten chunks, the widest the
donor's G2_EXPAND_MAX_CHUNKS = 16 gate allows).  Both helpers now take an
optional hidden width (default HIDDEN, so the accepted G2-1 arms and their
assertions are untouched) and the case list gains exactly one real-width case,
(512 experts, top-10, 4 tokens, hidden 2560), with the same on/off subprocess
arms, the same bit-for-bit cross-arm and repeat-call comparisons and the same
torch NVFP4 reference; the small-shape cases, the stride-separation assertions
and the >32-token fallback case keep what they were.  The width reaches the
subprocess through the same run_case(*args) tuple as everything else, so the
arm and its reference cannot drift apart, and the case still has to satisfy the
host gate for the folded kernel to appear at all (2560 % 256 == 0 and
trips = 10 <= 16), i.e. it fails loudly rather than silently measuring the
fallback.

Not run here: room101 has no GPU, no CUDA toolkit and no torch/pytest, so the
SM120 build and every pytest arm (including the numerical ones) are for the
thegrid run.  Checked on room101: python -m py_compile of the new test, a
symbol cross-check of every imported helper, a line-by-line diff of the folded
row branch against the donor patch (only the documented blockIdx/gridDim
rewiring, the re-wraps under ColumnLimit 100 and the added asserts differ), a
check that the five kernel-name matchers cannot match each other (so no arm can
pass vacuously), greps proving no stride-fusion code leaked in (the only
layout_info mention in the diff is the comment saying it is absent), and
git grep on the exact base proving the folded-arm assertion is the one that
fails on b9fa889, where the kernel and the switch do not exist.
